Every good path between two cities must have an edge corresponding to a shortcut because there are no copies of a railway edge in the path. Therefore, for each city, there are n - 1 edges that we can find the shortest path from it, and if we add a shortcut for each edge, the distance between that city and any other city will be one more based on what our shortest distance was without shortcuts. Thus, for every city, there are n - i (where i is the city index) new edges to add as a shortcut. We can sum along every city to find out the number of new edges between any one of the cities with an index greater than one and one of the first city.

Here's the python solution based on the above logic:

