We start by creating an adjacency list representation of Dušan's railway map and attempting to find the best combination of shortcuts we can add while keeping the shortcutting diameter less than or equal to k. We use DFS and by collecting all the nodes in one communication, we can find what would be the best length of a path between cities, when the radius and diameter are fixed.

For each distance between the trees, the new distances to each node in the external trees (`copy`) can be reduced. In fact, by solving the problem on the largest component of the second component of the shortest path from the root to a node in the adjacency table of the tree, you can attribute the length of that distance back to the parts opposite each tree, and you can divide that divided value by two regardless of its positioning. In this way we calculate the largest possible distance between any two cities, and subtract it from k, if k is greater than this value, we are left with the total number of shortcuts that were added.

